{"metric":"small_universes","source":[{"metric":"small_universes","system":"horn","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"26b40baa364b738e"},{"metric":"small_universes","system":"torus","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"54445dc5e116138e"},{"metric":"small_universes","system":"torus_conformal","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"09a0167636b12d42"}],"views":[{"id":"torus","label":"Comoving time","box":[-0.45,2.0208,-0.3,3.4416],"system":"torus","input":"A cubic torus of dust, its three periods one length $L$: $a = (3ct/2L)^{2/3}$, checked to solve this spacetime's own $G^x{}_x = 0$, so the Hubble radius $3ct/2$ is $L$ when $a = 1$.","caption":["The plane of $t$ and $x$ of a torus of dust, each point in the diagram a 2-torus of area $a^2L^2$. The metric on it is $a^2(-d\\eta^2 + dx^2)$ with $\\eta = 2L\\sqrt{a}$, and a conformal factor changes no null direction, so $p, q = \\arctan((\\eta \\mp x)/L)$ bring it into Minkowski's triangle, where the lines $x = 0$ and $x = L$ are one surface of the torus.","The big bang is spacelike, the segment along the bottom, and since $x$ is bounded every world line and every light ray ends at the one point $i^+$, $t \\to \\infty$. A ray that leaves the diagram through $x = L$ comes back in through $x = 0$ at the same $t$, so the ray from the bang laps the universe again and again, and no particle horizon stays between two galaxies for long."],"layers":[{"kind":"fill","class":"region","points":[[0.0,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,0.0],[1.5708,